window.__courseChapter("v1-08", {"v1-08-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Model Training** (Vol I). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, Purpose", "md": "Inference computes a forward pass as data flows through the network and a prediction emerges. Training adds a backward pass and an optimizer step, retains intermediate state, and repeats that work across many examples and optimization steps. Optimizers may also maintain momentum and variance estimates whose storage exceeds the model weights, so memory rather than arithmetic often decides what fits on a device."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "From One Step to a Training Run", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "Training-Time Law", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "Computation and Memory Costs of Training", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "Pipeline Architecture", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "Identifying Bottlenecks", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Pipeline Optimizations", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Scaling Training Systems", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**Training-time law**", "In the macroscopic D·A·M product form of the iron law, training work is about 6PD FLOPs (D tokens times 6P FLOPs per token) and run time is that work over peak rate times utilization, so every point of utilization recovered at constant power removes time, energy, and dollars in the same proportion, while memory sits outside the law as a feasibility constraint."], ["**Model FLOPs utilization**", "MFU is useful model FLOPs per step over peak times measured step time and is the honest estimate of utilization, whereas the busy time a device monitor reports is only an upper bound, high for a memory-stalled job and low for a data-starved one."], ["**Training memory at scale**", "Mixed-precision Adam holds 16 bytes per parameter of static state (eight times the FP16 inference weights) before any activation, and only the activation term grows with batch and sequence length, which is what pushes a large model past one accelerator's memory."], ["**Mixed precision**", "Running eligible matrix products in FP16 or BF16 raises the attainable peak and halves activation bytes but leaves the 16 B/param static state unchanged, FP16 needs dynamic loss scaling to keep gradients above its normal floor while BF16 usually does not, and the speedup is bounded by the work that stays off the matrix units."], ["**Checkpointing and accumulation**", "Activation checkpointing recomputes segment interiors to cut activation memory from O(L) to O(sqrt L) for about one extra forward pass, and gradient accumulation builds a large effective batch from micro-batches that fit, reproducing the large-batch gradient only when losses are normalized consistently."], ["**Profile to find the limiting term**", "Which technique helps depends on which resource is saturated, so the workflow is timeline first, then hardware counters, then one targeted fix and a re-profile, feasibility comes before speed, and a fix on a stage that is not the bottleneck buys nothing."], ["**Fine-tuning scope**", "Training scope decides which memory terms exist: full fine-tuning of an 8B model needs about 128 GB of static state, while adapters such as LoRA shrink gradients and optimizer state to well under one percent of parameters, and activations still follow the backward path through every frozen layer."], ["**Training input pipeline**", "Training throughput is the minimum of host preparation rate, host-link rate, and compute rate, and overlap makes the step cost the maximum of the stage times but cannot raise the slowest stage, so workers, prefetch depth, and pinned memory are sized against measured demand."], ["**IO-aware attention**", "FlashAttention computes exact attention tile by tile in on-chip memory with online-softmax statistics and recomputes tiles in backward, dropping stored attention state from quadratic to linear in sequence length and cutting memory traffic without changing model FLOPs."], ["**Single-node data parallelism**", "Data parallelism replicates the model and averages gradients with AllReduce, whose per-device traffic depends on parameter count rather than batch, so splitting a fixed batch across one node's accelerators raises the share of unhidden synchronization unless it overlaps with backward."], ["**Sharding within one node**", "When per-replica state does not fit, ZeRO stages shard optimizer state, then gradients, then parameters across one node's accelerators, trading memory for communication, and layerwise model parallelism pays a pipeline bubble unless micro-batches are pipelined."], ["**Training checkpoints**", "A training checkpoint saves persistent state (weights, master copy, optimizer moments) plus scheduler, RNG, data position, and scaler state for exact resume, and checkpoint cadence trades save stalls against replayed work, so fault tolerance is a throughput decision."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$T_{\\text{train}} = \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\times \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$$\n\n$$\\mathbf{A}^{(l)} = f\\left(\\mathbf{A}^{(l-1)}\\mathbf{W}^{(l)} + \\mathbf{b}^{(l)}\\right)$$\n\n$$\\text{GELU}(x) = x \\cdot \\Phi(x) = x \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\left[1 + \\text{erf}\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\right]$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v1-08-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "Training-time law", "back": "In the macroscopic D·A·M product form of the iron law, training work is about 6PD FLOPs (D tokens times 6P FLOPs per token) and run time is that work over peak rate times utilization, so every point of utilization recovered at constant power removes time, energy, and dollars in the same proportion, while memory sits outside the law as a feasibility constraint."}, {"front": "Model FLOPs utilization", "back": "MFU is useful model FLOPs per step over peak times measured step time and is the honest estimate of utilization, whereas the busy time a device monitor reports is only an upper bound, high for a memory-stalled job and low for a data-starved one."}, {"front": "Training memory at scale", "back": "Mixed-precision Adam holds 16 bytes per parameter of static state (eight times the FP16 inference weights) before any activation, and only the activation term grows with batch and sequence length, which is what pushes a large model past one accelerator's memory."}, {"front": "Mixed precision", "back": "Running eligible matrix products in FP16 or BF16 raises the attainable peak and halves activation bytes but leaves the 16 B/param static state unchanged, FP16 needs dynamic loss scaling to keep gradients above its normal floor while BF16 usually does not, and the speedup is bounded by the work that stays off the matrix units."}, {"front": "Checkpointing and accumulation", "back": "Activation checkpointing recomputes segment interiors to cut activation memory from O(L) to O(sqrt L) for about one extra forward pass, and gradient accumulation builds a large effective batch from micro-batches that fit, reproducing the large-batch gradient only when losses are normalized consistently."}, {"front": "Profile to find the limiting term", "back": "Which technique helps depends on which resource is saturated, so the workflow is timeline first, then hardware counters, then one targeted fix and a re-profile, feasibility comes before speed, and a fix on a stage that is not the bottleneck buys nothing."}, {"front": "Fine-tuning scope", "back": "Training scope decides which memory terms exist: full fine-tuning of an 8B model needs about 128 GB of static state, while adapters such as LoRA shrink gradients and optimizer state to well under one percent of parameters, and activations still follow the backward path through every frozen layer."}, {"front": "Training input pipeline", "back": "Training throughput is the minimum of host preparation rate, host-link rate, and compute rate, and overlap makes the step cost the maximum of the stage times but cannot raise the slowest stage, so workers, prefetch depth, and pinned memory are sized against measured demand."}, {"front": "IO-aware attention", "back": "FlashAttention computes exact attention tile by tile in on-chip memory with online-softmax statistics and recomputes tiles in backward, dropping stored attention state from quadratic to linear in sequence length and cutting memory traffic without changing model FLOPs."}, {"front": "Single-node data parallelism", "back": "Data parallelism replicates the model and averages gradients with AllReduce, whose per-device traffic depends on parameter count rather than batch, so splitting a fixed batch across one node's accelerators raises the share of unhidden synchronization unless it overlaps with backward."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Training-time law**?", "options": ["When per-replica state does not fit, ZeRO stages shard optimizer state, then gradients, then parameters across one node's accelerators, trading memory for communication, and layerwise model parallelism pays a pipeline bubble unless micro-batches are pipelined.", "Mixed-precision Adam holds 16 bytes per parameter of static state (eight times the FP16 inference weights) before any activation, and only the activation term grows with batch and sequence length, which is what pushes a large model past one accelerator's memory.", "In the macroscopic D·A·M product form of the iron law, training work is about 6PD FLOPs (D tokens times 6P FLOPs per token) and run time is that work over peak rate times utilization, so every point of utilization recovered at constant power removes time, energy, and dollars in the same proportion, while memory sits outside the law as a feasibility constraint.", "Activation checkpointing recomputes segment interiors to cut activation memory from O(L) to O(sqrt L) for about one extra forward pass, and gradient accumulation builds a large effective batch from micro-batches that fit, reproducing the large-batch gradient only when losses are normalized consistently."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Training-time law:** In the macroscopic D·A·M product form of the iron law, training work is about 6PD FLOPs (D tokens times 6P FLOPs per token) and run time is that work over peak rate times utilization, so every point of utilization recovered at constant power removes time, energy, and dollars in the same proportion, while memory sits outside the law as a feasibility constraint.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Training-time law itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Model FLOPs utilization**?", "options": ["Mixed-precision Adam holds 16 bytes per parameter of static state (eight times the FP16 inference weights) before any activation, and only the activation term grows with batch and sequence length, which is what pushes a large model past one accelerator's memory.", "MFU is useful model FLOPs per step over peak times measured step time and is the honest estimate of utilization, whereas the busy time a device monitor reports is only an upper bound, high for a memory-stalled job and low for a data-starved one.", "Activation checkpointing recomputes segment interiors to cut activation memory from O(L) to O(sqrt L) for about one extra forward pass, and gradient accumulation builds a large effective batch from micro-batches that fit, reproducing the large-batch gradient only when losses are normalized consistently.", "A training checkpoint saves persistent state (weights, master copy, optimizer moments) plus scheduler, RNG, data position, and scaler state for exact resume, and checkpoint cadence trades save stalls against replayed work, so fault tolerance is a throughput decision."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Model FLOPs utilization:** MFU is useful model FLOPs per step over peak times measured step time and is the honest estimate of utilization, whereas the busy time a device monitor reports is only an upper bound, high for a memory-stalled job and low for a data-starved one.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Model FLOPs utilization itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Training memory at scale**?", "options": ["Mixed-precision Adam holds 16 bytes per parameter of static state (eight times the FP16 inference weights) before any activation, and only the activation term grows with batch and sequence length, which is what pushes a large model past one accelerator's memory.", "Training throughput is the minimum of host preparation rate, host-link rate, and compute rate, and overlap makes the step cost the maximum of the stage times but cannot raise the slowest stage, so workers, prefetch depth, and pinned memory are sized against measured demand.", "MFU is useful model FLOPs per step over peak times measured step time and is the honest estimate of utilization, whereas the busy time a device monitor reports is only an upper bound, high for a memory-stalled job and low for a data-starved one.", "Data parallelism replicates the model and averages gradients with AllReduce, whose per-device traffic depends on parameter count rather than batch, so splitting a fixed batch across one node's accelerators raises the share of unhidden synchronization unless it overlaps with backward."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**Training memory at scale:** Mixed-precision Adam holds 16 bytes per parameter of static state (eight times the FP16 inference weights) before any activation, and only the activation term grows with batch and sequence length, which is what pushes a large model past one accelerator's memory.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Training memory at scale itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Mixed precision**?", "options": ["Training throughput is the minimum of host preparation rate, host-link rate, and compute rate, and overlap makes the step cost the maximum of the stage times but cannot raise the slowest stage, so workers, prefetch depth, and pinned memory are sized against measured demand.", "Running eligible matrix products in FP16 or BF16 raises the attainable peak and halves activation bytes but leaves the 16 B/param static state unchanged, FP16 needs dynamic loss scaling to keep gradients above its normal floor while BF16 usually does not, and the speedup is bounded by the work that stays off the matrix units.", "Activation checkpointing recomputes segment interiors to cut activation memory from O(L) to O(sqrt L) for about one extra forward pass, and gradient accumulation builds a large effective batch from micro-batches that fit, reproducing the large-batch gradient only when losses are normalized consistently.", "Training scope decides which memory terms exist: full fine-tuning of an 8B model needs about 128 GB of static state, while adapters such as LoRA shrink gradients and optimizer state to well under one percent of parameters, and activations still follow the backward path through every frozen layer."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Mixed precision:** Running eligible matrix products in FP16 or BF16 raises the attainable peak and halves activation bytes but leaves the 16 B/param static state unchanged, FP16 needs dynamic loss scaling to keep gradients above its normal floor while BF16 usually does not, and the speedup is bounded by the work that stays off the matrix units.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Mixed precision itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Checkpointing and accumulation**?", "options": ["In the macroscopic D·A·M product form of the iron law, training work is about 6PD FLOPs (D tokens times 6P FLOPs per token) and run time is that work over peak rate times utilization, so every point of utilization recovered at constant power removes time, energy, and dollars in the same proportion, while memory sits outside the law as a feasibility constraint.", "FlashAttention computes exact attention tile by tile in on-chip memory with online-softmax statistics and recomputes tiles in backward, dropping stored attention state from quadratic to linear in sequence length and cutting memory traffic without changing model FLOPs.", "Activation checkpointing recomputes segment interiors to cut activation memory from O(L) to O(sqrt L) for about one extra forward pass, and gradient accumulation builds a large effective batch from micro-batches that fit, reproducing the large-batch gradient only when losses are normalized consistently.", "Which technique helps depends on which resource is saturated, so the workflow is timeline first, then hardware counters, then one targeted fix and a re-profile, feasibility comes before speed, and a fix on a stage that is not the bottleneck buys nothing."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Checkpointing and accumulation:** Activation checkpointing recomputes segment interiors to cut activation memory from O(L) to O(sqrt L) for about one extra forward pass, and gradient accumulation builds a large effective batch from micro-batches that fit, reproducing the large-batch gradient only when losses are normalized consistently.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Checkpointing and accumulation itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["Training-time law: In the macroscopic D·A·M product form of the iron law, training work is about 6PD FLOPs (D tokens times 6P FLOPs per token) and run time is that work over peak rate times utilization, so every point of utilization recovered at constant power removes time, energy, and dollars in the same proportion, while memory sits outside the law as a feasibility constraint.", "Model FLOPs utilization: MFU is useful model FLOPs per step over peak times measured step time and is the honest estimate of utilization, whereas the busy time a device monitor reports is only an upper bound, high for a memory-stalled job and low for a data-starved one.", "Training memory at scale: Mixed-precision Adam holds 16 bytes per parameter of static state (eight times the FP16 inference weights) before any activation, and only the activation term grows with batch and sequence length, which is what pushes a large model past one accelerator's memory.", "Mixed precision: Running eligible matrix products in FP16 or BF16 raises the attainable peak and halves activation bytes but leaves the 16 B/param static state unchanged, FP16 needs dynamic loss scaling to keep gradients above its normal floor while BF16 usually does not, and the speedup is bounded by the work that stays off the matrix units."]}], "terms": [], "id": "v1-08-00-overview", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v1-08-01-from-one-step-to-a-training-run": {"title": "From One Step to a Training Run", "summary": "Calculate the static mixed-precision Adam training-state footprint from weights, gradients, master weights, and optimizer state.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["From One Step to a Training Run", "Running cases: GPT-2 XL and ResNet-50"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **From One Step to a Training Run** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate the static mixed-precision Adam training-state footprint from weights, gradients, master weights, and optimizer state\n- Distinguish memory-capacity failures from numerical-instability failures in mixed-precision training\n- Identify the systems term for trading backward recomputation against forward activation storage"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § From One Step to a Training Run", "md": "A training run is one training step repeated millions of times, so every byte a step holds and every millisecond it spends is multiplied by the length of the run. Each step reads a batch of examples, computes a forward pass and a loss, propagates gradients backward, and updates every parameter, and the run repeats that sequence until the model reaches its quality target."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Running cases: GPT-2 XL and ResNet-50\n\nPay attention to: training-vs-inference cost asymmetry and memory footprint decomposition; the scale of one training step against a whole training run; numerical-range overflow versus memory capacity exhaustion; activation rematerialization trade-offs."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A 7-billion-parameter model uses 14 GB of FP16 weights for inference and fits comfortably on a single 40 GB accelerator. Switching the same model to standard mixed-precision Adam training causes immediate out-of-memory failures even before activations are accounted for. Using the mixed-precision Adam accounting that the section previews, which factor is most directly responsible?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. In the section's standard mixed-precision Adam recipe, training state includes 14 GB of FP16 weights, 14 GB of FP16 gradients, 28 GB of FP32 master weights, and 56 GB of FP32 moment state, for about 112 GB before activations. The idea that Adam re-runs model passes confuses optimizer state with the forward/backward computation, and dataset caching is part of the input pipeline rather than resident model-training state on the accelerator.", "type": "single", "options": ["Adam internally re-runs the forward and backward passes, so the same weights must be stored several extra times during each step.", "Adam adds 14 GB of FP16 gradients, 28 GB of FP32 master weights, and 56 GB of FP32 first- and second-moment state, pushing the static training state to about 112 GB before activations.", "Adam requires the full training corpus to be cached in accelerator memory so batches can be reused across optimizer steps.", "Adam forces all model tensors into FP64 during backpropagation, which multiplies the weight footprint beyond device capacity."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the static mixed-precision Adam training-state footprint from weights, gradients, master weights, and optimizer state."]}, {"q": "Sudden NaN loss spikes during mixed-precision training are usually the same failure mode as an out-of-memory error, because both come from the GPU running out of memory during backpropagation.", "explanation": "False. An out-of-memory failure is a capacity problem that triggers an allocation error, while NaNs indicate numerical instability somewhere in the training computation. Mixed precision can contribute to numerical issues, but a NaN spike is not the same diagnosis as memory exhaustion.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish memory-capacity failures from numerical-instability failures in mixed-precision training."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate the static mixed-precision Adam training-state footprint from weights, gradients, master weights, and optimizer state.", "You can now distinguish memory-capacity failures from numerical-instability failures in mixed-precision training.", "You can now identify the systems term for trading backward recomputation against forward activation storage."]}], "id": "v1-08-01-from-one-step-to-a-training-run", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-02-training-time-law": {"title": "Training-Time Law", "summary": "Apply the training-time law to identify utilization as the immediate profiling target when workload size and peak-throughput convention are fixed.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Training-Time Law"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Training-Time Law** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the training-time law to identify utilization as the immediate profiling target when workload size and peak-throughput convention are fixed\n- Classify mixed-precision training by its direct target term ($R_{\\text{peak}}$) in the training-time law\n- Explain what the simplified training-time law captures and why it does not by itself localize the cause of low-throughput execution\n- Distinguish compute-bound dense training from regimes where memory capacity or bandwidth is the limiting constraint\n- Trace the historical sequence linking major training-system milestones to the constraints they exposed"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § Training-Time Law", "md": "Every later estimate in the chapter starts from how long the run takes, and the training-time law fixes that first. GPT-2 XL's run needs about …. Converting that operation count into days on an accelerator, and every loss of time into a factor engineering can recover, requires splitting training time into physical factors, with every wasted second showing up as a lower utilization factor."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: applying the training-time law to measured telemetry; mapping optimizations to O, R_peak, and eta_hw; scope conditions and failure modes of the simplified Iron Law; workload characterization (dense compute-bound GPT-2 vs memory-bound DLRM)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain the scope conditions under which the simplified training-time law $T_{\\text{train}} = \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\times \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$ is useful, and identify why the formula alone cannot diagnose a slow small-batch debugging run.", "md": "The equation is useful as an accounting identity: it relates total work, a chosen peak-throughput reference, and effective achieved throughput into one training-time estimate. But because the end-to-end utilization term can already include stalls from launches, transfers, synchronization, and underfilled kernels, the formula alone does not reveal which mechanism caused a slow run. In a small-batch debug session, the problem is not that the law becomes invalid; it is that a low measured utilization could come from many overheads, so diagnosis still requires profiling rather than reading the formula by itself."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A training run has confirmed that input delivery and communication are off the critical path. The model FLOP count and the peak-throughput convention are being held fixed, yet profiling still reports only 38 percent effective end-to-end utilization relative to the stated peak. Under the training-time law $T_{\\text{train}} = \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\times \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$, which lever is the most direct target for the next round of profiling?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. In the current equation, $O$ and the chosen $R_{\\text{peak}}$ convention are fixed, so 38 percent effective end-to-end utilization relative to the stated peak points to the effective utilization term $\\eta_{\\text{hw}}$. That does not prove one unique cause, but it does justify profiling device execution for stalls, inefficient kernels, launch overhead, or other losses inside the end-to-end throughput term. Reducing $O$ may shorten total training time, but it answers a different question than why this execution reaches only 38 percent of the chosen peak.", "type": "single", "options": ["Hardware utilization $\\eta_{\\text{hw}}$, because with $O$ and the relevant $R_{\\text{peak}}$ convention fixed, the remaining gap points to how efficiently kernels are being executed end to end.", "Peak throughput $R_{\\text{peak}}$, because low effective end-to-end utilization relative to the stated peak automatically means the hardware peak specification is too small for the workload.", "Total operations $O$, because once data and communication are hidden, utilization is no longer relevant to training time.", "Dataset size, because shrinking the number of tokens directly raises effective end-to-end utilization relative to the stated peak even when execution efficiency is unchanged."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: apply the training-time law to identify utilization as the immediate profiling target when workload size and peak-throughput convention are fixed."]}, {"q": "When a team transitions a model from FP32 training to mixed-precision (FP16/BF16) on Tensor Core accelerators without changing the model architecture, batch size, or dataset, which term of the training-time law ($T_{\\text{train}} = \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\times \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$) is most directly improved?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The chapter's optimization matching table assigns mixed precision (FP16/BF16) to peak throughput ($R_{\\text{peak}}$). Tensor Cores execute reduced-precision matrix multiplications at several times the theoretical FLOP/s ceiling of FP32 ALUs. The claim regarding total operations $O$ is incorrect because the algorithmic graph and number of multiply-accumulate operations remain identical. The utilization claim is incorrect because $\\eta_{\\text{hw}}$ measures efficiency relative to the available peak, and precision changes do not inherently eliminate data loader bubbles. The data volume claim is false because precision does not reduce sample requirements.", "type": "single", "options": ["Total operations $O$, because lower numerical precision halves the number of multiplications required in each matrix multiplication.", "Hardware utilization $\\eta_{\\text{hw}}$, because mixed precision automatically eliminates all GPU pipeline bubbles and CPU data stalls.", "Peak throughput $R_{\\text{peak}}$, because Tensor Cores provide a substantially higher theoretical FLOP/s ceiling for reduced-precision matrix operations compared to standard FP32 execution units.", "Data volume $D_{\\text{tokens}}$, because lower precision requires fewer training tokens for the loss to converge."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: classify mixed-precision training by its direct target term ($R_{\\text{peak}}$) in the training-time law."]}, {"q": "All large deep learning models are primarily compute-bound, so training speed is always governed mainly by Tensor Core TFLOP/s.", "explanation": "False. Dense transformer training often spends much of its work in matrix multiplications, but other workloads can be limited by memory capacity or bandwidth instead. The section's DLRM example is dominated by large embedding tables, where fitting and moving the state can matter more than raw dense-math throughput.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish compute-bound dense training from regimes where memory capacity or bandwidth is the limiting constraint."]}, {"q": "Place the following milestones in chronological order, reflecting how training-system constraints evolved:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) Backpropagation is popularized for multilayer neural networks, (4) AlexNet demonstrates that deep neural network training maps effectively to GPUs, (1) Transformers shift sequence modeling toward high-throughput dense matrix multiplication and large activation footprints, (3) Large-scale foundation models such as GPT-3 make utilization and multi-accelerator scaling central engineering concerns, (5) IO-aware FlashAttention appears as a response to attention memory-bandwidth limits. This sequence matters because each later systems response addresses a constraint exposed by the earlier stage: GPU acceleration follows practical deep learning, transformers amplify activation and attention costs, frontier-scale models make utilization and scaling decisive, and FlashAttention targets the resulting IO bottleneck in attention.", "type": "order", "items": ["Backpropagation is popularized for multilayer neural networks.", "AlexNet demonstrates that deep neural network training maps effectively to GPUs.", "Transformers shift sequence modeling toward high-throughput dense matrix multiplication and large activation footprints.", "Large-scale foundation models such as GPT-3 make utilization and multi-accelerator scaling central engineering concerns.", "IO-aware FlashAttention appears as a response to attention memory-bandwidth limits."], "hints": ["Think about: trace the historical sequence linking major training-system milestones to the constraints they exposed."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply the training-time law to identify utilization as the immediate profiling target when workload size and peak-throughput convention are fixed.", "You can now classify mixed-precision training by its direct target term ($R_{\\text{peak}}$) in the training-time law.", "You can now explain what the simplified training-time law captures and why it does not by itself localize the cause of low-throughput execution.", "You can now distinguish compute-bound dense training from regimes where memory capacity or bandwidth is the limiting constraint."]}], "id": "v1-08-02-training-time-law", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-03-computation-and-memory-costs-of": {"title": "Computation and Memory Costs of Training", "summary": "Compare GEMM and GEMV using arithmetic intensity and operand reuse in the accelerator memory hierarchy.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Computation and Memory Costs of Training", "Forward and backward computation", "Optimizers and their memory", "Backpropagation and activation memory", "Arithmetic intensity"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Computation and Memory Costs of Training** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Compare GEMM and GEMV using arithmetic intensity and operand reuse in the accelerator memory hierarchy\n- Calculate whether SGD or Adam fits within a fixed HBM budget when common training state is already accounted for\n- Identify the definition and significance of the ridge point in roofline model analysis\n- Order the operations of a training iteration based on their mathematical and data dependencies\n- Use ridge-point comparison to classify an attention implementation and select the matching traffic-reduction optimization direction"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § Computation and Memory Costs of Training", "md": "@Sec-neural-computation established *what* neural network operations compute and *why* they enable learning; what they cost at scale depends on where their tensors live. In a model such as GPT-2, the tensors participating in a single training step far exceed the 30 to 50 MB of on-chip SRAM available on a modern accelerator. Executing a layer's matrix multiplication PreactivationMatrix= ActivationMatrixWeightMatrix therefore moves matrix tiles continuously between two tiers of memory."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Forward and backward computation\n- Optimizers and their memory\n- Backpropagation and activation memory\n- Arithmetic intensity\n\nPay attention to: BLAS Level 3 GEMM vs BLAS Level 2 GEMV arithmetic intensity and hardware efficiency; optimizer state memory accounting for Adam versus SGD; roofline ridge point definition and arithmetic intensity; causal dependencies of backpropagation operations."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A team trains a 7-billion-parameter model on accelerators with 80 GB of HBM each. Weights, gradients, activations, and common workspaces already occupy 64 GB per accelerator, and this total already includes any FP32 master weights if the recipe uses them. The model is unsharded per device. Using the section's optimizer-memory accounting, explain the systems trade-off between choosing plain SGD and Adam for this run.", "md": "Adam adds two FP32 moment tensors, or 8 bytes per parameter, so a 7B-parameter model needs about 56 GB more optimizer state. That pushes the per-device total from 64 GB to about 120 GB, which exceeds an 80 GB accelerator before any sharding decision is considered, whereas plain SGD adds no auxiliary optimizer state and still fits at 64 GB. The practical consequence is that this choice is first a capacity question; once a feasible optimizer is chosen, the team should then measure time to target quality rather than assume one optimizer will always converge faster."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why do batched matrix-matrix multiplications (GEMM, BLAS Level 3) usually achieve much higher accelerator utilization in neural network training than matrix-vector operations (GEMV, BLAS Level 2)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Under the section's idealized traffic analysis, GEMM performs many more FLOPs per byte moved because tiles of weights and activations are reused across multiple outputs, so its arithmetic intensity can scale upward with problem size. GEMV offers far less reuse, so moving bytes can dominate execution. The claim that GEMM avoids gradients is wrong because training uses matrix multiplies heavily in backward as well as forward, and the CPU-synchronization explanation confuses low arithmetic intensity with an unsupported execution model.", "type": "single", "options": ["Matrix-matrix operations avoid gradient computation during backpropagation, so they save both memory traffic and arithmetic work.", "Under the ideal traffic model in the section, GEMM reuses loaded operands across many outputs, so arithmetic intensity can grow with matrix size, whereas GEMV offers much less reuse and tends to stay near constant arithmetic intensity.", "Matrix-vector operations cannot run efficiently on GPUs because every layer result must be synchronized back to the CPU before the next layer starts.", "Batched matrix-matrix operations reduce the model's parameter count, which is why they keep more of the network in HBM."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: compare GEMM and GEMV using arithmetic intensity and operand reuse in the accelerator memory hierarchy."]}, {"q": "For a transformer with hidden dimension d_model = 768 and 12 heads (d_head = 64), the section's restricted accounting gives the materialized attention-score computation an arithmetic intensity of about d_head/2 = 32 FLOP/byte. If the target accelerator is the reference H100, whose ridge point is about \\$295 FLOP/byte, what does that imply and what optimization direction best matches the section?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. With an estimated intensity of \\$32\\text{ FLOP/byte}$ against the H100's ridge point of about \\$295\\text{ FLOP/byte}$, the roofline classifies this implementation as memory-bandwidth-limited under the stated traffic model. That does not prove a measured kernel already saturates bandwidth or that hardware upgrades can never help, but it does indicate that reducing score-matrix traffic is the matching optimization direction. The network answer is irrelevant because this is a local attention-kernel analysis, and shrinking batch size does not address the materialized-attention IO term the section highlights.", "type": "single", "options": ["The kernel is compute-bound, so increasing peak TFLOP/s is the main expected lever under this model.", "The kernel is latency-bound, so shrinking the batch is the main way to move it onto the compute roof.", "The kernel is network-bound, so the right first response is a faster inter-node AllReduce fabric.", "The kernel falls on the memory-bandwidth side of the roofline under this accounting, so reducing materialized score traffic through IO-aware tiling is the appropriate optimization direction."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: use ridge-point comparison to classify an attention implementation and select the matching traffic-reduction optimization direction."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now compare GEMM and GEMV using arithmetic intensity and operand reuse in the accelerator memory hierarchy.", "You can now calculate whether SGD or Adam fits within a fixed HBM budget when common training state is already accounted for.", "You can now identify the definition and significance of the ridge point in roofline model analysis.", "You can now order the operations of a training iteration based on their mathematical and data dependencies."]}], "id": "v1-08-03-computation-and-memory-costs-of", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-04-pipeline-architecture": {"title": "Pipeline Architecture", "summary": "Identify the three major subsystems of a training pipeline architecture and explain the systems rationale for this decomposition.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Pipeline Architecture", "Pipeline stages and accelerator bubbles", "Data pipeline", "Forward and backward pass", "Parameter updates", "Training state and checkpoints"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Pipeline Architecture** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Identify the three major subsystems of a training pipeline architecture and explain the systems rationale for this decomposition\n- Apply the pipeline bottleneck model to identify the slowest comparable stage as the limit on end-to-end throughput\n- Analyze why preprocessing latency can dominate input delivery even when the PCIe transfer itself is short\n- Evaluate why changing batch size can alter optimization behavior even when hardware throughput improves\n- Identify the systems mechanism (pinned memory) used for asynchronous host-to-device transfers"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § Pipeline Architecture", "md": "A training step is not a single operation but a sequence of dependent stages: data must be loaded before computation can begin, forward passes must complete before backward passes start, and gradients must be computed before parameters can update. The speed of the slowest stage determines the speed of the entire system. Without pipelining, these stages serialize, idling the accelerator while the host prepares data or transfers batches across the bus."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Pipeline stages and accelerator bubbles\n- Data pipeline\n- Forward and backward pass\n- Parameter updates\n- Training state and checkpoints\n\nPay attention to: subsystem decomposition of training pipelines; pipeline bottleneck model and end-to-end throughput limits; CPU-side preprocessing and GIL bottlenecks; batch size scaling and learning rate interactions."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why CPU-side tokenization and data augmentation can severely bottleneck GPU training even when the resulting tensor transfer across PCIe takes less than one millisecond.", "md": "A fast PCIe copy only shows that the transfer stage is short once a batch already exists; it says nothing about how long the CPU needed to create that batch. If CPU preprocessing takes 65 ms and GPU compute takes 80 ms, then with proper overlap the next batch can still be ready in time, but without overlap or with an unstable host service interval the GPU can see bubbles despite the sub-millisecond transfer. The practical consequence is that busy-time fractions alone are not peak arithmetic efficiency and do not prove a specific worker count; what matters is whether preprocessing can consistently supply the next batch before compute needs it."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Which set of subsystems defines the chapter's high-level training system architecture, and what systems engineering advantage does this decomposition provide?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The training architecture decomposes the system into three interconnected subsystems: the data pipeline (ingestion, transformation, batching), the training loop (forward pass, loss, backward pass, optimizer update), and the evaluation pipeline (validation metrics on held-out data). This separation enables engineers to diagnose bottlenecks by subsystem resource demands (host CPU/disk for data, accelerator compute/HBM for training loop, and periodic synchronization pauses for evaluation). The storage controller and compiler option describes compiler infrastructure rather than runtime training systems. The tokenizer and registry option describes MLOps lifecycle tools. The gradient aggregator option lists distributed implementation components rather than end-to-end training subsystems.", "type": "single", "options": ["Storage controller, compiler intermediate representation, and runtime execution engine; this separates hardware target code generation from storage layout.", "Data pipeline, training loop, and evaluation pipeline; this separates distinct resource profiles (CPU/storage I/O, accelerator compute/memory, and periodic validation) so bottlenecks can be isolated at subsystem interfaces.", "Tokenizer, hyperparameter optimizer, and model registry; this organizes the model deployment lifecycle around developer interfaces.", "Gradient aggregator, parameter server, and checkpoint restorer; this decomposes cloud service microservices."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: identify the three major subsystems of a training pipeline architecture and explain the systems rationale for this decomposition."]}, {"q": "In a profiled training pipeline, CPU data preprocessing sustains \\$4 GB/s on the same logical batch representation that host-to-device transfer and GPU consumption are being compared against. Host-to-device PCIe transfer sustains \\$32 GB/s, and GPU compute consumes data at an equivalent rate of \\$12 GB/s. Under the section's ideal-overlap pipeline model, what determines end-to-end throughput, and what is the best first engineering action?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. In the section's pipeline model, comparable stage rates are combined through a minimum: the slowest stage caps throughput even if transfer and compute are faster. Here preprocessing at \\$4 GB/s is the limiting constraint, so the first goal is to raise input-side service rate and remove that bottleneck. An answer based on averaging ignores how pipelines work, and upgrading the PCIe or compute stage, neither of which limits throughput, does not change a system still waiting on preprocessing.", "type": "single", "options": ["The average of the three rates, so the effective throughput is \\$16 GB/s and all stages should be tuned evenly.", "The PCIe transfer rate, because every batch must cross the bus before any compute can happen.", "The minimum rate, \\$4 GB/s from preprocessing, so the first action is to reduce the input-side bottleneck before tuning faster stages.", "The GPU compute rate, because training pipelines are ultimately limited by accelerator execution speed rather than host stages."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: apply the pipeline bottleneck model to identify the slowest comparable stage as the limit on end-to-end throughput."]}, {"q": "Scaling the training batch size by \\$8× while keeping the learning rate and schedule unchanged guarantees the same model quality in less wall-clock time.", "explanation": "False. A larger batch changes both the number of updates per epoch and the gradient statistics seen by the optimizer, so the old learning-rate schedule may no longer be appropriate. The section says validation is needed after a large batch-size change, and retuning may be needed rather than guaranteed.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why changing batch size can alter optimization behavior even when hardware throughput improves."]}, {"q": "In the chapter's staged system pipeline framework, what constitutes an \"accelerator bubble\" and what is the primary diagnostic step to resolve it?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. An accelerator bubble is defined as idle or underutilized accelerator time resulting from a throughput mismatch across the four pipeline stages (Data Loading & Preprocessing, Host-to-Device Transfer, Forward/Backward Compute, and Parameter Synchronization). Resolving bubbles requires profiling each stage to locate the limiting constraint and applying overlapping techniques such as asynchronous prefetching. The assertion of intentional synchronization misinterprets a performance inefficiency as a design feature. The SRAM cache eviction option confuses low-level cache misses with macro pipeline stalls. The ridge-point loss option misapplies roofline terminology to pipeline-level time allocation.", "type": "single", "options": ["An intentional synchronization barrier inserted by automatic differentiation to ensure memory coherence across gradient passes; resolved by disabling graph checks.", "An on-chip SRAM cache eviction event caused by large transformer sequence lengths; resolved by switching to smaller embedding tables.", "A loss of arithmetic intensity when batch sizes fall below the hardware ridge point; resolved by forcing FP64 accumulation.", "Silicon idleness where accelerator ALUs wait for data loading, host-to-device PCIe transfer, or parameter synchronization; resolved by profiling the four pipeline stages to identify and overlap the slowest stage."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the systems definition of accelerator bubbles across the four pipeline stages and identify the diagnostic procedure to eliminate them."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now identify the three major subsystems of a training pipeline architecture and explain the systems rationale for this decomposition.", "You can now apply the pipeline bottleneck model to identify the slowest comparable stage as the limit on end-to-end throughput.", "You can now analyze why preprocessing latency can dominate input delivery even when the PCIe transfer itself is short.", "You can now evaluate why changing batch size can alter optimization behavior even when hardware throughput improves."]}], "id": "v1-08-04-pipeline-architecture", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-05-identifying-bottlenecks": {"title": "Identifying Bottlenecks", "summary": "Differentiate Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) from raw GPU busy utilization and explain which overheads MFU exposes.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Identifying Bottlenecks"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Identifying Bottlenecks** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Differentiate Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) from raw GPU busy utilization and explain which overheads MFU exposes\n- Apply D·A·M reasoning to profile signatures while distinguishing likely bottlenecks from confirmed diagnoses\n- Order the systematic profile-diagnose-fix-reprofile optimization cycle\n- Select the appropriate class of profiler (kernel-level counters versus timeline traces) based on the granularity of the performance investigation\n- Analyze staged-pipeline signals to distinguish likely input starvation from other host-side causes of accelerator bubbles"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § Identifying Bottlenecks", "md": "An accelerator can report near-100 percent activity in `nvidia-smi` while spending half its cycles stalled on memory traffic, padding, or recomputing discarded activations. Knowing what each operation costs on paper does not show which resource actually saturates during a run, and raw device busy-time percentages cannot separate useful training arithmetic from overhead. Model FLOPs utilization (MFU) supplies that comparison by measuring achieved model arithmetic against the hardware's peak rate."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: MFU versus raw hardware utilization percentage; D·A·M bottleneck classification and profiler signatures; systematic profile-diagnose-fix-reprofile workflow; profiler tool selection (Nsight Compute vs Nsight Systems / PyTorch Profiler)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: An engineer profiles two training workloads. Workload A shows 92 percent GPU busy time, near-saturated HBM bandwidth, low CPU activity, and continuous kernel timelines. Workload B shows 20 percent GPU busy time with regular 50 ms idle gaps, low HBM bandwidth, and 100 percent CPU utilization. Apply the D·A·M taxonomy to classify each workload's likely bottleneck and state the next optimization category for each.", "md": "Workload A supports a bandwidth-bound hypothesis on the Machine side: kernels are running continuously and HBM is heavily used, so the next step is to confirm that critical kernels are limited by memory traffic and then target traffic reduction or fusion. Workload B is consistent with a Data-side bottleneck only if the GPU gaps correlate with batch preparation rather than host dispatch or synchronization, so the next step is stage correlation and then input-pipeline optimization if starvation is confirmed. The practical consequence is that D·A·M categories are diagnostic guides, not exclusive labels you can assign from one metric alone."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why is Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) a more reliable metric than raw GPU busy percentage (reported by tools like `nvidia-smi`) when evaluating training systems efficiency?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Raw GPU utilization measures the percentage of time GPU execution units or kernels are active. A GPU can show 95% utilization while spending half its time stalling on memory bandwidth or recomputing activations during gradient checkpointing. In contrast, MFU ($\\text{MFU} = \\frac{O_{\\text{model}}}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\times T_{\\text{step}}}$) includes only the useful forward and backward model FLOPs in the numerator, explicitly penalizing recomputation, padding FLOPs, memory stalls, and pipeline bubbles. MFU is an accelerator metric, not a CPU metric. MFU is not fixed to 100%; reported values depend on workload, hardware, precision, and the FLOP-counting convention. MFU measures hardware efficiency, not loss convergence per dollar.", "type": "single", "options": ["MFU is measured directly from host CPU clock cycles, eliminating GPU driver instrumentation overhead.", "MFU is mathematically fixed to 100% on any healthy accelerator cluster regardless of software overhead.", "MFU counts only the theoretical forward and backward FLOPs required by the model architecture divided by the product of peak hardware throughput and measured step time, whereas raw GPU busy percentage also counts time spent on recomputation, padding, and memory stalls outside that model-FLOP numerator.", "MFU measures training loss convergence speed per dollar rather than floating-point operations."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: differentiate Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) from raw GPU busy utilization and explain which overheads MFU exposes."]}, {"q": "An engineering team suspects that an individual custom LayerNorm kernel has poor arithmetic intensity and low register occupancy, while another team suspects their DataLoader is causing host-to-device PCIe transfer stalls. Which combination of profiling tool classes from the chapter is best suited to investigate each respective issue?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The chapter distinguishes two classes of profilers. Kernel-level GPU profilers record per-kernel counters such as achieved memory throughput and arithmetic-unit occupancy, making them right for the custom LayerNorm kernel. Framework-level and system-level timeline profilers (for example PyTorch Profiler or NVIDIA Nsight Systems) record timeline traces capturing CPU thread execution, CUDA API dispatches, PCIe memory copies, and GPU stream synchronization, making them ideal for diagnosing DataLoader and PCIe stalls. `nvidia-smi` provides coarse device-wide polling rather than kernel-level roofline analysis. Linux `top` monitors host OS processes, not GPU internal instruction pipelines.", "type": "single", "options": ["A kernel-level GPU profiler with per-kernel counters for the arithmetic intensity and occupancy analysis; a framework-level or system-level timeline profiler for the DataLoader and PCIe transfers.", "`nvidia-smi` for kernel-level arithmetic intensity; TensorBoard loss curves for DataLoader PCIe transfer stalls.", "NVIDIA Nsight Compute for cluster-wide inter-node network bandwidth; PyTorch Profiler for register allocation analysis.", "Linux `top` command for GPU kernel instruction analysis; NVIDIA Nsight Systems for CPU register spill tracking."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: select the appropriate class of profiler (kernel-level counters versus timeline traces) based on the granularity of the performance investigation."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A team's profiler shows the GPU at 18 percent utilization with periodic drops to zero, while one CPU core is pinned near 100 percent and the PCIe link is mostly idle. Using the section's staged-pipeline framework, explain how to diagnose the bubble's source and what fix should be targeted.", "md": "This pattern suggests the input side of the staged pipeline may be starving the accelerator, because the GPU has bubbles while host work is active and PCIe is not the limiting stage. To confirm the source, correlate the GPU idle gaps with batch-preparation time versus host dispatch or synchronization delays; if the gaps line up with preprocessing, then the right fix is input-pipeline work such as additional workers or prefetching. The practical implication is that high CPU usage alone is not enough to blame tokenization or the GIL with certainty—the stage correlation must come first."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now differentiate Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) from raw GPU busy utilization and explain which overheads MFU exposes.", "You can now apply D·A·M reasoning to profile signatures while distinguishing likely bottlenecks from confirmed diagnoses.", "You can now order the systematic profile-diagnose-fix-reprofile optimization cycle.", "You can now select the appropriate class of profiler (kernel-level counters versus timeline traces) based on the granularity of the performance investigation."]}], "id": "v1-08-05-identifying-bottlenecks", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-06-pipeline-optimizations": {"title": "Pipeline Optimizations", "summary": "Calculate the steady-state throughput interval of an overlapped training pipeline from its stage times.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Pipeline Optimizations", "Systematic optimization framework", "Data prefetching and overlapping", "Mixed-precision training", "FlashAttention: IO-aware attention optimization", "Gradient accumulation and checkpointing", "GPT-2 optimization walkthrough"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Pipeline Optimizations** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate the steady-state throughput interval of an overlapped training pipeline from its stage times\n- Explain how IO-aware tiling and backward recomputation reduce attention memory traffic without changing attention mathematics\n- Justify FP32 master weights and selected FP32 reductions in the conventional FP16 mixed-precision training recipe\n- Analyze the memory behavior of gradient accumulation across multiple micro-batches\n- Identify loss scaling as the technique used to reduce FP16 gradient underflow risk"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § Pipeline Optimizations", "md": "Even well-designed training pipelines rarely approach hardware peak throughput without targeted optimization: at a typical model-FLOP utilization of … to …, an H100 rated at … delivers only …--… of useful model arithmetic. These derived scenario values reflect the gaps between peak and realized throughput reported across published large-model systems. Systems such as SuperNeurons show one version of this problem, in which memory-management bottlenecks prevent larger networks from training efficiently unless the runtime actively optimizes activation storage and transfer."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Systematic optimization framework\n- Data prefetching and overlapping\n- Mixed-precision training\n- FlashAttention: IO-aware attention optimization\n- Gradient accumulation and checkpointing\n- GPT-2 optimization walkthrough\n\nPay attention to: asynchronous prefetching and CUDA stream double buffering; FlashAttention IO-aware tiling, online softmax, and backward recomputation; mixed precision numerical stability, FP32 master weights, and selective FP32 reductions; gradient accumulation memory scaling mechanics."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain how FlashAttention computes exact self-attention with substantially lower memory footprint than standard attention, and describe what its backward pass does instead of reading a full stored score matrix from HBM.", "md": "FlashAttention keeps the same attention mathematics, with possible floating-point rounding differences, but changes where intermediate work lives: it processes query, key, and value tiles in on-chip SRAM and maintains the softmax statistics online instead of materializing the full $S \\times S$ score matrix in HBM. That cuts saved activation storage and reduces off-chip traffic tied to those score blocks rather than implying a linear law for all HBM IO. In backward, it recomputes the needed tiled score and probability blocks from saved inputs and normalization statistics rather than loading a full stored score matrix, so the systems gain comes from less HBM traffic, not from changing the attention mathematics."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "For each batch, a training step has three dependent stages in a serial baseline: data preprocessing ($T_{\\text{prep}} = 30\\text{ ms}$), host-to-device transfer ($T_{\\text{xfer}} = 10\\text{ ms}$), and GPU compute ($T_{\\text{comp}} = 50\\text{ ms}$). Different batches can occupy independent resources and overlap with correct handoff. If the team implements steady-state overlapping with prefetching and double buffering, what iteration interval is theoretically possible after fill and before drain?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The serial baseline takes \\$30 + 10 + 50 = 90 ms per batch, but ideal steady-state overlap changes the limiting interval to max(30, 10, 50) = 50 ms. That is a throughput improvement to \\$90/50 = 1.8×, not a promise that the latency of one isolated batch falls to 50 ms from startup. The \\$10 ms answer uses the fastest stage instead of the slowest, and the no-overlap answer ignores the purpose of prefetching and copy/compute concurrency.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$40 ms, because preprocessing and transfer replace compute once overlap is enabled.", "\\$50 ms, because in ideal steady state the interval is bounded by the slowest overlapped stage rather than the serial sum.", "\\$10 ms, because all stages run concurrently and the fastest one sets the pace.", "\\$90 ms, because DMA transfers cannot overlap useful GPU computation."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the steady-state throughput interval of an overlapped training pipeline from its stage times."]}, {"q": "Why does the chapter's conventional FP16 mixed-precision recipe keep FP32 master weights and perform selected reductions in FP32 instead of forcing every part of training into 16-bit precision?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The section's FP16 recipe keeps FP32 master weights because an update can be too small to change a stored FP16 weight after rounding even when the update itself is representable; preserving the update in FP32 avoids losing it across many steps. The same section also notes that selected reductions are often kept in FP32 because wider accumulation improves numerical behavior, though it does not guarantee stability everywhere. The FP64 and host-RAM claims invent hardware requirements the chapter does not make, and autodiff does not require a purely FP32 graph representation.", "type": "single", "options": ["Tensor Cores cannot execute backpropagation unless gradients are stored in FP64.", "FP16 allocations can lock the accelerator memory bus unless the master weights are moved to host RAM.", "Automatic differentiation requires symbolic derivatives to be represented only in FP32.", "Very small parameter updates can be rounded away when applied directly to FP16 weights, and selected reductions benefit from the wider range or precision of FP32 accumulation."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: justify FP32 master weights and selected FP32 reductions in the conventional FP16 mixed-precision training recipe."]}, {"q": "Gradient accumulation increases the memory footprint on the accelerator linearly with the accumulation factor $K$ because all $K$ micro-batches' intermediate activations must remain in GPU memory simultaneously until the optimizer step.", "explanation": "False. Gradient accumulation executes forward and backward passes sequentially across $K$ micro-batches, accumulating (summing) only the parameter gradients in a single fixed-size gradient buffer. Saved backward tensors for each micro-batch can be released after its backward pass when the graph is not retained; other references or cached tensors may extend storage lifetime. Consequently, activation memory is bounded by the size of a single micro-batch ($B_{\\text{micro}}$), allowing systems to emulate an effective batch size of $K \\times B_{\\text{micro}}$ without increasing activation memory.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the memory behavior of gradient accumulation across multiple micro-batches."]}, {"q": "In FP16 mixed-precision training, the loss is multiplied before backpropagation so the resulting gradients are shifted into a safer numeric range, then divided back down before the optimizer update. This technique is known as loss ____.", "explanation": "scaling. Loss scaling multiplies the loss before backward so the resulting gradients are less likely to underflow in FP16, then unscales them before the optimizer step.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["scaling"], "hints": ["Think about: identify loss scaling as the technique used to reduce FP16 gradient underflow risk."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A team enables mixed precision on a transformer model and observes compute time drop by 45 percent, but total step time decreases by only 10 percent because the GPU now shows large idle gaps. Explain what likely happened in the system pipeline and what the team should do next.", "md": "The compute optimization removed part of the old critical path, but the new timeline shows that another stage is now exposed because the GPU is spending meaningful time idle. Those gaps are not proof of a data-loader problem by themselves: they could come from input waits, dispatch overhead, synchronization, or another serial stage. The right next step is to re-profile and correlate the gaps with pipeline stages so the next optimization targets the new bottleneck rather than assuming mixed precision failed."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate the steady-state throughput interval of an overlapped training pipeline from its stage times.", "You can now explain how IO-aware tiling and backward recomputation reduce attention memory traffic without changing attention mathematics.", "You can now justify FP32 master weights and selected FP32 reductions in the conventional FP16 mixed-precision training recipe.", "You can now analyze the memory behavior of gradient accumulation across multiple micro-batches."]}], "id": "v1-08-06-pipeline-optimizations", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-07-scaling-training-systems": {"title": "Scaling Training Systems", "summary": "Select data parallelism as the first multi-GPU baseline when memory already fits on each device and the main goal is throughput.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Scaling Training Systems", "Single-node multi-GPU training", "Scaling beyond a single node", "When to scale: The physical ceiling"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Scaling Training Systems** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Select data parallelism as the first multi-GPU baseline when memory already fits on each device and the main goal is throughput\n- Calculate the per-GPU bytes sent in Ring AllReduce and interpret its scaling with device count\n- Analyze how communication overhead limits scaling efficiency as distributed training spans more accelerators\n- Identify the pipeline bubble pathology of naive pipeline parallelism and explain how micro-batch schedules mitigate it\n- Sequence the chapter's single-machine-first process for deciding when distributed scaling is actually justified"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § Scaling Training Systems", "md": "The GPT-2 walkthrough fits the batch-32 step on one H100 and finishes the run in about …. One accelerator cannot finish sooner, and some models cannot fit at all. Spread across the … accelerators of one server with perfect scaling, the same run would take about …. A 70-billion-parameter model needs about … for its FP16 weights alone and far more for full training state, which no single accelerator holds, motivating sharding, offload, or multiple devices."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Single-node multi-GPU training\n- Scaling beyond a single node\n- When to scale: The physical ceiling\n\nPay attention to: Data Parallelism vs Model Parallelism decision boundaries; Ring AllReduce communication volume and scaling asymptotic properties; communication overhead and scaling efficiency degradation; pipeline parallelism bubble overhead and micro-batch mitigation."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain what communication overhead is in distributed training, and analyze why adding more GPUs can lead to diminishing throughput returns.", "md": "Communication overhead is the time spent synchronizing gradients or exchanging partitioned activations and tensors instead of doing local model computation. As more GPUs are added, ideal compute work per device can fall, but communication latency, transfer volume, and incomplete overlap do not disappear, so synchronization becomes a larger fraction of each step. The practical consequence is that scaling efficiency drops once communication and coordination consume enough of the step that extra devices save less compute time than they add in communication cost."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team trains a 1.5-billion-parameter model that already fits on one 80 GB GPU with batch size 16 and its Adam training state, but one epoch still takes 12 days. The team has an 8-GPU node available. Which strategy is the best first baseline, and why?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. When the full model and training state already fit on one device, data parallelism is the natural first baseline because each GPU can run the local forward and backward pass without introducing the extra sequential dependencies of pipeline parallelism or the frequent collectives of tensor partitioning. An answer based on splitting layers or tensors solves a memory problem the scenario does not currently have. Sharding may still become useful later, but it is not the first throughput baseline when replication already fits.", "type": "single", "options": ["Tensor parallelism, because splitting every weight matrix across the 8 GPUs is always the fastest way to improve training throughput.", "Pipeline parallelism, because dividing layers across devices is the default first step whenever a model is a transformer.", "Data parallelism such as DDP, because the model already fits per device and replication avoids unnecessary pipeline bubbles or intra-layer communication as the initial throughput baseline.", "Full parameter sharding first, because any multi-GPU run should avoid keeping complete replicas even when memory is sufficient."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: select data parallelism as the first multi-GPU baseline when memory already fits on each device and the main goal is throughput."]}, {"q": "In distributed data parallel training with $N$ GPUs and a model with $P$ parameters whose gradients use $B_{\\text{bytes}}$ bytes each, what is the number of bytes sent by each GPU during a Ring AllReduce?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. In a ring AllReduce, each GPU participates in a scatter-reduce phase and an all-gather phase, and the bytes sent per GPU total \\$2 \\times \\frac{N-1}{N} \\times P \\times B_{\\text{bytes}}$. That expression tends toward \\$2 P B_{\\text{bytes}}$ as $N$ becomes large, so the per-GPU sent volume stays bounded rather than growing linearly with the cluster size. The broadcast-style answer describes a different and less efficient mental model of synchronization.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$2 \\times \\frac{N-1}{N} \\times P \\times B_{\\text{bytes}}$, which approaches \\$2 P B_{\\text{bytes}}$ as $N$ grows.", "$N \\times P \\times B_{\\text{bytes}}$, because each GPU must broadcast the full gradient to every other GPU.", "$\\frac{P \\times B_{\\text{bytes}}}{N^2}$, because the ring divides both the model and the communication quadratically.", "Zero, because GPU replicas synchronize through shared CPU memory rather than explicit data movement."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the per-GPU bytes sent in Ring AllReduce and interpret its scaling with device count."]}, {"q": "In pipeline model parallelism, partitioning a network linearly across $K$ stages without micro-batching (naive pipeline parallelism) achieves 100 percent GPU utilization because downstream devices can compute backward gradients while upstream devices run forward passes.", "explanation": "False. In naive pipeline parallelism without micro-batching, downstream devices must wait idle until upstream devices complete their forward passes, and upstream devices must wait idle until downstream devices compute backward loss gradients. This introduces massive idle pipeline bubbles where device utilization collapses to $\\approx 1/K$. Practical pipeline parallelism systems (like GPipe and 1F1B in PipeDream) mitigate this by splitting batches into multiple small micro-batches and interleaving forward and backward passes to fill the pipeline.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: identify the pipeline bubble pathology of naive pipeline parallelism and explain how micro-batch schedules mitigate it."]}, {"q": "Arrange the following engineering steps in the chapter's preferred order for deciding whether to scale beyond one device, assuming the initial bounds do not already prove that single-device training is infeasible:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (3) Establish a single-device baseline and quantify the current bottleneck, (4) Apply relevant local optimizations such as mixed precision, prefetching, checkpointing, or accumulation, (1) Re-evaluate whether memory or time limits still justify scaling, (2) Add distributed parallelism or sharding if the remaining limit still requires it. This order matters because the chapter's scaling discipline uses baseline measurement first, then local fixes targeted to the observed constraint, then a new decision about whether a hard limit remains. Jumping straight to distributed scaling can add communication and operational complexity before the single-device bottleneck is even understood.", "type": "order", "items": ["Establish a single-device baseline and quantify the current bottleneck.", "Apply relevant local optimizations such as mixed precision, prefetching, checkpointing, or accumulation.", "Re-evaluate whether memory or time limits still justify scaling.", "Add distributed parallelism or sharding if the remaining limit still requires it."], "hints": ["Think about: sequence the chapter's single-machine-first process for deciding when distributed scaling is actually justified."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now select data parallelism as the first multi-GPU baseline when memory already fits on each device and the main goal is throughput.", "You can now calculate the per-GPU bytes sent in Ring AllReduce and interpret its scaling with device count.", "You can now analyze how communication overhead limits scaling efficiency as distributed training spans more accelerators.", "You can now identify the pipeline bubble pathology of naive pipeline parallelism and explain how micro-batch schedules mitigate it."]}], "id": "v1-08-07-scaling-training-systems", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-08-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Distinguish the predictable systems costs of scaling parameter count from the uncertain effect on final model quality.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-08", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fallacies and Pitfalls** in *Model Training* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Distinguish the predictable systems costs of scaling parameter count from the uncertain effect on final model quality\n- Explain why batch-size scaling can invalidate a previously successful optimization recipe even when hardware utilization looks strong\n- Recognize optimizing kernels before profiling the input pipeline as a major operational pitfall in training optimization\n- Evaluate why communication overhead and operational complexity both limit the developer-velocity gains of distributed training"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Model Training, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "The approach developed throughout this chapter quantifies costs through the iron law, diagnoses bottlenecks through profiling, applies targeted optimizations, and scales only when necessary. The following fallacies and pitfalls are the errors that most often waste training compute."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Summary\n\nPay attention to: model scaling without matched data scaling (cost guarantee vs validation uncertainty); hyperparameter transfer failures under batch size scaling; pipeline neglect and data-side blind spots; distributed training complexity and non-linear velocity scaling."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why directly transferring hyperparameters such as learning rate and weight decay from a batch-size 512 baseline to a batch-size 4,096 distributed run can cause training to underperform, even when raw GPU busy time stays above 90 percent.", "md": "An eightfold batch increase means eight times fewer parameter updates per fixed epoch, and it also changes the gradient statistics seen by the optimizer, so the old hyperparameters no longer operate in the same regime. High GPU busy time only shows that hardware is active; it does not prove that the optimizer is making equally effective progress per unit data. The practical response is to validate the learning-rate schedule, warmup, and target quality metric on the larger-batch run instead of assuming the small-batch recipe transfers unchanged."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team scales a dense model from 7 billion to 20 billion parameters while keeping the same training-token budget, precision recipe, and optimizer structure. Under the chapter's fallacies discussion, what is the most defensible conclusion?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Under the chapter's dense-model accounting with fixed precision and optimizer assumptions, a larger parameter count clearly raises memory and leading FLOP requirements, so systems cost goes up in a predictable direction. But the scientific outcome is not guaranteed: keeping the same token budget does not prove either improvement or collapse in validation quality. The \"must improve\" answer mistakes model size for a universal quality guarantee, while the CPU answer invents a hardware consequence the chapter does not state.", "type": "single", "options": ["Validation accuracy must improve in proportion to parameter count because the larger model can represent more functions.", "The run will necessarily move to CPU execution because 20B parameters cannot be trained on accelerators.", "The larger model guarantees worse accuracy because fixed-token training always causes catastrophic interference at higher parameter counts.", "The training-state and compute budgets rise substantially under the same dense-model accounting, but model quality is not guaranteed to improve because it still depends on data, optimization, and evaluation."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish the predictable systems costs of scaling parameter count from the uncertain effect on final model quality."]}, {"q": "Which engineering mistake exemplifies the input-pipeline pitfall described in the chapter's fallacies and pitfalls?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The chapter's pitfall of optimizing kernels before profiling the input pipeline occurs when engineers focus exclusively on accelerator-side compute optimizations (such as custom CUDA kernels, mixed precision, or Tensor Core utilization) while neglecting end-to-end profiling. If the upstream data pipeline (CPU tokenization, disk I/O, or augmentation) is bottlenecked, the GPU will starve, rendering kernel-level speedups useless at the system level. BF16, DataLoader worker and pinned-memory settings, and checkpointing are candidate interventions whose value depends on the diagnosed constraint and validation results.", "type": "single", "options": ["Using BF16 mixed precision instead of FP16 on NVIDIA Ampere GPUs.", "Spending weeks hand-tuning custom CUDA attention kernels to achieve 95% peak arithmetic throughput, only to discover in production that the GPU sits idle 50% of the time waiting for single-threaded Python CPU tokenization.", "Setting `pin_memory=True` and `num_workers=8` in a PyTorch DataLoader.", "Checkpointing activations every $\\sqrt{N_L}$ layers to reduce peak memory."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: recognize optimizing kernels before profiling the input pipeline as a major operational pitfall in training optimization."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A team proposes moving a single-GPU training job to a 16-GPU cluster to achieve a \\$16\\times$ reduction in development cycle time. Explain two reasons why distributed training rarely delivers a linear \\$16\\times$ improvement in developer velocity.", "md": "First, distributed execution adds communication and synchronization overhead, so throughput rarely scales linearly with GPU count even when the code is correct. Second, developer velocity depends on more than throughput: distributed jobs bring extra debugging, launch, checkpointing, and failure-handling overhead that can delay iteration even if step time improves. The practical consequence is that cluster scaling should be justified by measured end-to-end iteration gains, not by hardware count alone."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now distinguish the predictable systems costs of scaling parameter count from the uncertain effect on final model quality.", "You can now explain why batch-size scaling can invalidate a previously successful optimization recipe even when hardware utilization looks strong.", "You can now recognize optimizing kernels before profiling the input pipeline as a major operational pitfall in training optimization.", "You can now evaluate why communication overhead and operational complexity both limit the developer-velocity gains of distributed training."]}], "id": "v1-08-08-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v1-08", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-08-99-test": {"title": "Test: Model Training", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-08-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "When a team transitions a model from FP32 training to mixed-precision (FP16/BF16) on Tensor Core accelerators without changing the model architecture, batch size, or dataset, which term of the training-time law ($T_{\\text{train}} = \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\times \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$) is most directly improved?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The chapter's optimization matching table assigns mixed precision (FP16/BF16) to peak throughput ($R_{\\text{peak}}$). Tensor Cores execute reduced-precision matrix multiplications at several times the theoretical FLOP/s ceiling of FP32 ALUs. The claim regarding total operations $O$ is incorrect because the algorithmic graph and number of multiply-accumulate operations remain identical. The utilization claim is incorrect because $\\eta_{\\text{hw}}$ measures efficiency relative to the available peak, and precision changes do not inherently eliminate data loader bubbles. The data volume claim is false because precision does not reduce sample requirements.", "type": "single", "options": ["Total operations $O$, because lower numerical precision halves the number of multiplications required in each matrix multiplication.", "Hardware utilization $\\eta_{\\text{hw}}$, because mixed precision automatically eliminates all GPU pipeline bubbles and CPU data stalls.", "Peak throughput $R_{\\text{peak}}$, because Tensor Cores provide a substantially higher theoretical FLOP/s ceiling for reduced-precision matrix operations compared to standard FP32 execution units.", "Data volume $D_{\\text{tokens}}$, because lower precision requires fewer training tokens for the loss to converge."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: classify mixed-precision training by its direct target term ($R_{\\text{peak}}$) in the training-time law."]}, {"q": "Why does the chapter's conventional FP16 mixed-precision recipe keep FP32 master weights and perform selected reductions in FP32 instead of forcing every part of training into 16-bit precision?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The section's FP16 recipe keeps FP32 master weights because an update can be too small to change a stored FP16 weight after rounding even when the update itself is representable; preserving the update in FP32 avoids losing it across many steps. The same section also notes that selected reductions are often kept in FP32 because wider accumulation improves numerical behavior, though it does not guarantee stability everywhere. The FP64 and host-RAM claims invent hardware requirements the chapter does not make, and autodiff does not require a purely FP32 graph representation.", "type": "single", "options": ["Tensor Cores cannot execute backpropagation unless gradients are stored in FP64.", "FP16 allocations can lock the accelerator memory bus unless the master weights are moved to host RAM.", "Automatic differentiation requires symbolic derivatives to be represented only in FP32.", "Very small parameter updates can be rounded away when applied directly to FP16 weights, and selected reductions benefit from the wider range or precision of FP32 accumulation."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: justify FP32 master weights and selected FP32 reductions in the conventional FP16 mixed-precision training recipe."]}, {"q": "Place the following milestones in chronological order, reflecting how training-system constraints evolved:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) Backpropagation is popularized for multilayer neural networks, (4) AlexNet demonstrates that deep neural network training maps effectively to GPUs, (1) Transformers shift sequence modeling toward high-throughput dense matrix multiplication and large activation footprints, (3) Large-scale foundation models such as GPT-3 make utilization and multi-accelerator scaling central engineering concerns, (5) IO-aware FlashAttention appears as a response to attention memory-bandwidth limits. This sequence matters because each later systems response addresses a constraint exposed by the earlier stage: GPU acceleration follows practical deep learning, transformers amplify activation and attention costs, frontier-scale models make utilization and scaling decisive, and FlashAttention targets the resulting IO bottleneck in attention.", "type": "order", "items": ["Backpropagation is popularized for multilayer neural networks.", "AlexNet demonstrates that deep neural network training maps effectively to GPUs.", "Transformers shift sequence modeling toward high-throughput dense matrix multiplication and large activation footprints.", "Large-scale foundation models such as GPT-3 make utilization and multi-accelerator scaling central engineering concerns.", "IO-aware FlashAttention appears as a response to attention memory-bandwidth limits."], "hints": ["Think about: trace the historical sequence linking major training-system milestones to the constraints they exposed."]}, {"q": "An engineering team suspects that an individual custom LayerNorm kernel has poor arithmetic intensity and low register occupancy, while another team suspects their DataLoader is causing host-to-device PCIe transfer stalls. Which combination of profiling tool classes from the chapter is best suited to investigate each respective issue?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The chapter distinguishes two classes of profilers. Kernel-level GPU profilers record per-kernel counters such as achieved memory throughput and arithmetic-unit occupancy, making them right for the custom LayerNorm kernel. Framework-level and system-level timeline profilers (for example PyTorch Profiler or NVIDIA Nsight Systems) record timeline traces capturing CPU thread execution, CUDA API dispatches, PCIe memory copies, and GPU stream synchronization, making them ideal for diagnosing DataLoader and PCIe stalls. `nvidia-smi` provides coarse device-wide polling rather than kernel-level roofline analysis. Linux `top` monitors host OS processes, not GPU internal instruction pipelines.", "type": "single", "options": ["A kernel-level GPU profiler with per-kernel counters for the arithmetic intensity and occupancy analysis; a framework-level or system-level timeline profiler for the DataLoader and PCIe transfers.", "`nvidia-smi` for kernel-level arithmetic intensity; TensorBoard loss curves for DataLoader PCIe transfer stalls.", "NVIDIA Nsight Compute for cluster-wide inter-node network bandwidth; PyTorch Profiler for register allocation analysis.", "Linux `top` command for GPU kernel instruction analysis; NVIDIA Nsight Systems for CPU register spill tracking."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: select the appropriate class of profiler (kernel-level counters versus timeline traces) based on the granularity of the performance investigation."]}, {"q": "A team scales a dense model from 7 billion to 20 billion parameters while keeping the same training-token budget, precision recipe, and optimizer structure. Under the chapter's fallacies discussion, what is the most defensible conclusion?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Under the chapter's dense-model accounting with fixed precision and optimizer assumptions, a larger parameter count clearly raises memory and leading FLOP requirements, so systems cost goes up in a predictable direction. But the scientific outcome is not guaranteed: keeping the same token budget does not prove either improvement or collapse in validation quality. The \"must improve\" answer mistakes model size for a universal quality guarantee, while the CPU answer invents a hardware consequence the chapter does not state.", "type": "single", "options": ["Validation accuracy must improve in proportion to parameter count because the larger model can represent more functions.", "The run will necessarily move to CPU execution because 20B parameters cannot be trained on accelerators.", "The larger model guarantees worse accuracy because fixed-token training always causes catastrophic interference at higher parameter counts.", "The training-state and compute budgets rise substantially under the same dense-model accounting, but model quality is not guaranteed to improve because it still depends on data, optimization, and evaluation."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish the predictable systems costs of scaling parameter count from the uncertain effect on final model quality."]}, {"q": "Arrange the following engineering steps in the chapter's preferred order for deciding whether to scale beyond one device, assuming the initial bounds do not already prove that single-device training is infeasible:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (3) Establish a single-device baseline and quantify the current bottleneck, (4) Apply relevant local optimizations such as mixed precision, prefetching, checkpointing, or accumulation, (1) Re-evaluate whether memory or time limits still justify scaling, (2) Add distributed parallelism or sharding if the remaining limit still requires it. This order matters because the chapter's scaling discipline uses baseline measurement first, then local fixes targeted to the observed constraint, then a new decision about whether a hard limit remains. Jumping straight to distributed scaling can add communication and operational complexity before the single-device bottleneck is even understood.", "type": "order", "items": ["Establish a single-device baseline and quantify the current bottleneck.", "Apply relevant local optimizations such as mixed precision, prefetching, checkpointing, or accumulation.", "Re-evaluate whether memory or time limits still justify scaling.", "Add distributed parallelism or sharding if the remaining limit still requires it."], "hints": ["Think about: sequence the chapter's single-machine-first process for deciding when distributed scaling is actually justified."]}, {"q": "Sudden NaN loss spikes during mixed-precision training are usually the same failure mode as an out-of-memory error, because both come from the GPU running out of memory during backpropagation.", "explanation": "False. An out-of-memory failure is a capacity problem that triggers an allocation error, while NaNs indicate numerical instability somewhere in the training computation. Mixed precision can contribute to numerical issues, but a NaN spike is not the same diagnosis as memory exhaustion.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish memory-capacity failures from numerical-instability failures in mixed-precision training."]}, {"q": "Why is Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) a more reliable metric than raw GPU busy percentage (reported by tools like `nvidia-smi`) when evaluating training systems efficiency?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Raw GPU utilization measures the percentage of time GPU execution units or kernels are active. A GPU can show 95% utilization while spending half its time stalling on memory bandwidth or recomputing activations during gradient checkpointing. In contrast, MFU ($\\text{MFU} = \\frac{O_{\\text{model}}}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\times T_{\\text{step}}}$) includes only the useful forward and backward model FLOPs in the numerator, explicitly penalizing recomputation, padding FLOPs, memory stalls, and pipeline bubbles. MFU is an accelerator metric, not a CPU metric. MFU is not fixed to 100%; reported values depend on workload, hardware, precision, and the FLOP-counting convention. MFU measures hardware efficiency, not loss convergence per dollar.", "type": "single", "options": ["MFU is measured directly from host CPU clock cycles, eliminating GPU driver instrumentation overhead.", "MFU is mathematically fixed to 100% on any healthy accelerator cluster regardless of software overhead.", "MFU counts only the theoretical forward and backward FLOPs required by the model architecture divided by the product of peak hardware throughput and measured step time, whereas raw GPU busy percentage also counts time spent on recomputation, padding, and memory stalls outside that model-FLOP numerator.", "MFU measures training loss convergence speed per dollar rather than floating-point operations."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: differentiate Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) from raw GPU busy utilization and explain which overheads MFU exposes."]}, {"q": "For each batch, a training step has three dependent stages in a serial baseline: data preprocessing ($T_{\\text{prep}} = 30\\text{ ms}$), host-to-device transfer ($T_{\\text{xfer}} = 10\\text{ ms}$), and GPU compute ($T_{\\text{comp}} = 50\\text{ ms}$). Different batches can occupy independent resources and overlap with correct handoff. If the team implements steady-state overlapping with prefetching and double buffering, what iteration interval is theoretically possible after fill and before drain?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The serial baseline takes \\$30 + 10 + 50 = 90 ms per batch, but ideal steady-state overlap changes the limiting interval to max(30, 10, 50) = 50 ms. That is a throughput improvement to \\$90/50 = 1.8×, not a promise that the latency of one isolated batch falls to 50 ms from startup. The \\$10 ms answer uses the fastest stage instead of the slowest, and the no-overlap answer ignores the purpose of prefetching and copy/compute concurrency.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$40 ms, because preprocessing and transfer replace compute once overlap is enabled.", "\\$50 ms, because in ideal steady state the interval is bounded by the slowest overlapped stage rather than the serial sum.", "\\$10 ms, because all stages run concurrently and the fastest one sets the pace.", "\\$90 ms, because DMA transfers cannot overlap useful GPU computation."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the steady-state throughput interval of an overlapped training pipeline from its stage times."]}, {"q": "In FP16 mixed-precision training, the loss is multiplied before backpropagation so the resulting gradients are shifted into a safer numeric range, then divided back down before the optimizer update. This technique is known as loss ____.", "explanation": "scaling. Loss scaling multiplies the loss before backward so the resulting gradients are less likely to underflow in FP16, then unscales them before the optimizer step.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["scaling"], "hints": ["Think about: identify loss scaling as the technique used to reduce FP16 gradient underflow risk."]}, {"q": "Scaling the training batch size by \\$8× while keeping the learning rate and schedule unchanged guarantees the same model quality in less wall-clock time.", "explanation": "False. A larger batch changes both the number of updates per epoch and the gradient statistics seen by the optimizer, so the old learning-rate schedule may no longer be appropriate. The section says validation is needed after a large batch-size change, and retuning may be needed rather than guaranteed.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why changing batch size can alter optimization behavior even when hardware throughput improves."]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v1-08-99-test", "chapter": "v1-08", "minutes": 10}});
